{
 "title": tr("1.5 Refinery processes — physical and chemical changes", "1.5 Refinery processes — physical તથા chemical changes"),
 "items": [
  {"h": tr("1.5.1 Refinery processes involving physical changes — separation without new molecules",
            "1.5.1 Physical changes વાળા refinery processes — નવા molecules વગર separation"),
   "syllabus_item": "1.5.1",
   "body": {
"en": """<h4>Definition / principle</h4><p>In a <b>physical (separation) process</b> the molecules themselves do not change; a mixture is separated because its components differ in a physical property — boiling point, solubility, melting point or molecular size.</p><h4>Working — sequence and reason</h4><ol><li><b>Dehydration and desalting:</b> water and salt are washed out of the crude first, to prevent fouling and corrosion in every hot unit that follows.</li><li><b>Atmospheric distillation:</b> the heated crude is separated by boiling range into gases/LPG, naphtha, kerosene, diesel and atmospheric residue.</li><li><b>Vacuum distillation:</b> the residue is distilled under vacuum so that heavy lube and gas-oil fractions boil without cracking.</li><li><b>Solvent extraction and dewaxing:</b> a solvent dissolves either the wanted or the unwanted part (aromatics from lube oil; oil from wax), and the two liquid phases are separated.</li><li><b>Adsorption/clay treatment:</b> colour- and gum-forming impurities stick to a solid surface and are removed.</li></ol><h4>Key points and data</h4><ul><li>Driving property: volatility (distillation), solubility (extraction), crystallisation (dewaxing), surface attraction (adsorption).</li><li>No catalyst and no chemical reaction; the separated parts can, in principle, be re-mixed to the original feed.</li><li>Physical processes cannot create more petrol than the crude naturally contains — that limit is why chemical processes exist.</li></ul><h4>Qualification</h4><p>Physical separation is limited by equilibrium: one distillation stage or one extraction contact gives only a partial separation, so industrial units use many stages (a fractionating column, multi-stage extraction) to reach product specifications.</p>""",
"gu": """<h4>વ્યાખ્યા / સિદ્ધાંત</h4><p><b>Physical (separation) process</b> માં molecules પોતે બદલાતા નથી; mixture એટલા માટે છૂટું પડે છે કારણ કે તેના components કોઈ physical property માં જુદા છે — boiling point, solubility, melting point કે molecular size.</p><h4>Working — sequence and reason</h4><ol><li><b>Dehydration તથા desalting:</b> crude માંથી water તથા salt સૌથી પહેલાં ધોવાય છે, જેથી પછીના દરેક hot unit માં fouling તથા corrosion અટકે.</li><li><b>Atmospheric distillation:</b> ગરમ crude boiling range મુજબ gases/LPG, naphtha, kerosene, diesel તથા atmospheric residue માં છૂટું પડે છે.</li><li><b>Vacuum distillation:</b> residue vacuum હેઠળ distill થાય છે જેથી heavy lube તથા gas-oil fractions cracking વગર ઊકળે.</li><li><b>Solvent extraction તથા dewaxing:</b> solvent ઇચ્છિત કે અનિચ્છિત ભાગને ઓગાળે છે (lube oil માંથી aromatics; wax માંથી oil), અને બે liquid phases છૂટા પડે છે.</li><li><b>Adsorption/clay treatment:</b> colour- તથા gum-forming impurities solid surface પર ચોંટીને દૂર થાય છે.</li></ol><h4>મુખ્ય મુદ્દા તથા data</h4><ul><li>Driving property: volatility (distillation), solubility (extraction), crystallisation (dewaxing), surface attraction (adsorption).</li><li>Catalyst કે chemical reaction નહીં; છૂટા પડેલા ભાગો સિદ્ધાંતમાં ફરી ભેળવીને original feed બનાવી શકાય છે.</li><li>Physical processes crude માં કુદરતી રીતે હોય તેનાથી વધુ petrol બનાવી શકતા નથી — આ જ મર્યાદાને કારણે chemical processes અસ્તિત્વમાં છે.</li></ul><h4>મર્યાદા / Qualification</h4><p>Physical separation equilibrium થી મર્યાદિત છે: એક distillation stage કે એક extraction contact માત્ર partial separation આપે છે, તેથી industrial units product specifications સુધી પહોંચવા ઘણા stages વાપરે છે (fractionating column, multi-stage extraction).</p>"""}},
  {"h": tr("1.5.2 Refinery processes involving chemical changes — making new molecules",
            "1.5.2 Chemical changes વાળા refinery processes — નવા molecules બનાવવા"),
   "syllabus_item": "1.5.2",
   "body": {
"en": """<h4>Definition / principle</h4><p>In a <b>chemical (conversion) process</b> chemical bonds are broken or rearranged, so new molecules — and therefore new products — are formed. These processes exist to change both the <i>amount</i> and the <i>quality</i> of products beyond what distillation can give.</p><h4>Detailed theory and conditions</h4><p><b>Cracking</b> breaks large, low-value molecules into smaller petrol- and LPG-range molecules, thermally or with a catalyst (Unit III). <b>Reforming</b> rearranges naphtha molecules over a platinum catalyst into aromatics and branched molecules of much higher octane number, and produces hydrogen as a valuable by-product (Unit III). <b>Hydrotreating</b> reacts a fraction with hydrogen over a catalyst to remove sulfur, nitrogen and oxygen and to saturate unstable compounds (Units III–IV). Other chemical processes used in refineries include <b>isomerisation</b> (straight chains to branched, for octane), <b>alkylation</b> (combining isobutane and butylene to a high-octane petrol component) and <b>polymerisation</b> of light gases. Sweetening and solvent treatments that only remove or convert the most reactive sulfur compounds are covered in Unit IV.</p><h4>Comparison</h4><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Physical process</th><th>Chemical process</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Molecules</td><td>Unchanged, only separated</td><td>Broken/rearranged; new molecules formed</td></tr><tr><td>Can it increase petrol yield?</td><td>No — limited to crude content</td><td>Yes — heavy fractions are converted</td></tr><tr><td>Typical needs</td><td>Heat, vacuum, solvent</td><td>Catalyst, hydrogen, high temperature/pressure</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>Qualification</h4><p>Chemical conversion is not free improvement: it consumes hydrogen, catalyst and energy, makes some gas and coke as by-products, and needs strict safety control at high temperature and pressure. A refinery uses it only where the product value gained exceeds that cost.</p>""",
"gu": """<h4>વ્યાખ્યા / સિદ્ધાંત</h4><p><b>Chemical (conversion) process</b> માં chemical bonds તૂટે છે કે rearrange થાય છે, તેથી નવા molecules — અને તેથી નવા products — બને છે. આ processes distillation આપી શકે તેનાથી આગળ products નો <i>જથ્થો</i> તથા <i>quality</i> બંને બદલવા માટે છે.</p><h4>વિગતવાર Theory તથા Conditions</h4><p><b>Cracking</b> મોટા, ઓછા-મૂલ્યના molecules ને નાના petrol- તથા LPG-range molecules માં તોડે છે, thermally કે catalyst સાથે (Unit III). <b>Reforming</b> naphtha molecules ને platinum catalyst પર ખૂબ વધુ octane number વાળા aromatics તથા branched molecules માં rearrange કરે છે, અને મૂલ્યવાન by-product તરીકે hydrogen ઉત્પન્ન કરે છે (Unit III). <b>Hydrotreating</b> fraction ને catalyst પર hydrogen સાથે react કરી sulfur, nitrogen તથા oxygen દૂર કરે છે અને unstable compounds ને saturate કરે છે (Units III–IV). Refineries માં વપરાતા અન્ય chemical processes માં <b>isomerisation</b> (octane માટે straight chains ને branched માં), <b>alkylation</b> (isobutane તથા butylene જોડી high-octane petrol component) અને light gases નું <b>polymerisation</b> છે. ફક્ત સૌથી reactive sulfur compounds દૂર કરતા કે convert કરતા sweetening તથા solvent treatments Unit IV માં છે.</p><h4>Comparison</h4><table><thead><tr><th>મુદ્દો</th><th>Physical process</th><th>Chemical process</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Molecules</td><td>યથાવત, ફક્ત separated</td><td>તૂટે/rearrange થાય; નવા molecules બને</td></tr><tr><td>Petrol yield વધારી શકે?</td><td>ના — crude content સુધી મર્યાદિત</td><td>હા — heavy fractions convert થાય છે</td></tr><tr><td>સામાન્ય જરૂરિયાતો</td><td>Heat, vacuum, solvent</td><td>Catalyst, hydrogen, high temperature/pressure</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>મર્યાદા / Qualification</h4><p>Chemical conversion મફત improvement નથી: તે hydrogen, catalyst તથા energy વાપરે છે, by-products તરીકે થોડો gas તથા coke બનાવે છે, અને high temperature તથા pressure પર કડક safety control જોઈએ છે. Refinery તેનો ઉપયોગ ત્યારે જ કરે છે જ્યાં મળતું product value તે ખર્ચ કરતાં વધે.</p>"""}}
 ]
},
